# Project operating instructions

Repository: `{{repository}}`

Verified origin: `{{origin}}`

Instruction seed: [factory source `{{revision}}`]({{factory_url}}/tree/{{revision}})

Read `ENGINEERING.md` before implementation. These shared rules apply to Codex and Claude Code; follow applicable nested instructions. The factory is a separate harness; these local rules and pinned references provide this app's shared guidance.

- Implement the requested product or change. Choose components, runtime, hosting and verification from its needs; record important decisions and respect existing conventions.
- Lead design before substantial implementation: propose an approach and observable success, ask consequential questions promptly with a recommendation, infer routine reversible details and state material assumptions, and postpone only with a reason and a trigger before dependent work. Reuse prior answers; do not require a fully specified prompt or a universal intake form.
- Check the required repository/build/test/hosting/integration path and actual source/runtime revisions early. Separate verified capability from configuration or identity reads. Surface unavailable access/tooling before dependent implementation, with evidence and a concrete next action; do not silently substitute a scaffold for the agreed working solution.
- Execute the agreed sequence after design is settled. Bound access diagnostics (normally two minutes per blocked prerequisite), use supported command/connect timeouts and report the first failure promptly. During active work, give useful updates within a minute. Retry only with new evidence or a changed prerequisite; explain required platform approvals before waiting on them and keep observed failures separate from permission hypotheses.
- Tie status to artifacts: verified remote repository URL/visibility, product code/commit, remote push/PR, completed checks and a verified live user flow. Label local-only preparation, pending requests, skipped checks and unknown outcomes explicitly. Setup inspection and seeded instructions are not implementation or deployment evidence.
- Answer execution concerns before more tools. Honor explicit pause/stop/cancellation immediately, cancel pending work where safe and preserve unknown outcomes for reconciliation. Resume stopped work only on an explicit restart; a separate new task does not restart it.
- Validate untrusted inputs at the relevant boundary, constrain work and return safe errors. Apply authentication/authorization and data durability where required. Keep secrets out of source, client bundles and logs.
- Maintain the actual commands and operational contract in `ENGINEERING.md`. Pin relevant dependencies/tooling, commit lockfiles where applicable and use meaningful workload-appropriate checks and app-owned CI/CD. Verify real integration where components interact.
- Work in this repository. Preserve user files and dirty work, verify fetch/push origin before external writes and use the approved branch workflow. Do not force-push, change visibility or delete to recover.
- Reuse the human-approved scope for routine steps. Check provider identity/target immediately before actions. Scope, credentials/IAM, core exposure, production/protection or budget changes and required action-time approvals remain gates. These instructions grant no provider authority.
- Resolve exact targets within existing authorization and supply programmatic confirmation flags without asking the user to repeat routine names. Product/design questions can be asked before a concrete release-approval step. Stop only blocked actions, report pending waits/approvals and continue independent authorized work; never evade a tool/provider denial.
- For selected provider operations, use the verified factory runtime paths when available: `factory-aws` (role-only CLI or explicitly trusted SDK/IaC exec), `factory-gh`/`factory-git` (selected GitHub source), and `factory-vercel` (recorded API operation). Preserve private operation evidence and the approved scope; tool availability is not a grant.
- Apply source/run/project provenance and exact private resource inventory to hosting/infrastructure work. Keep credentials, state and private provider configuration out of source/logs. Do not load arbitrary `.env` as shell code. Keep factory execution credentials out of app CI; use reviewed app-owned authentication.
- Keep project continuity in this repo's `.factory/` registry and `ENGINEERING.md`. Use `factory-project open --repo {{repository}}` on continuation and apply verified activation. Initialize known targets before consequential writes; batch `factory-project checkpoint` at delivery milestones, then review/commit/push registry and app changes through the normal branch/PR workflow. Runtime evidence stays local until checkpointed. Public repos exclude confidential config/inventories; secrets, raw responses and raw IaC state stay outside Git. No central context repository is used.
- Reconcile unknown mutation outcomes before retries. Make source durable through authorized Git push or private export/restore before relying on a fresh task. Record actual results and remaining blockers.
- Maintain delivery state in `ENGINEERING.md` and approved private records: current goal, decisions/assumptions, postponed decisions and triggers, scope references, source/validation/deployment state, blockers and next actions. Preserve the objective across corrections and status questions. On continuation, verify actual state and reuse prior decisions; a fresh session does not itself invalidate them.

Keep these instructions and the app's engineering contract current as the project evolves.
